{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"casts-rtti","title":"Casts & RTTI","group":"Inheritance & Polymorphism","order":41,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">C++ replaces the all-powerful C cast with four named casts, each saying <em>what</em> you're doing. <code>dynamic_cast</code> and <code>typeid</code> use Run-Time Type Information.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>The four casts</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cast</th><th>Checked</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>static_cast&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>Compile time</td><td>Numeric conversions, <code>void*</code> → <code>T*</code>, up-casts, down-casts you <em>know</em> are valid, enum ↔ int</td></tr><tr><td><code>dynamic_cast&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><strong>Run time</strong></td><td>Safe down-cast/cross-cast in a polymorphic hierarchy; <code>nullptr</code> (pointers) or <code>std::bad_cast</code> (references) on failure</td></tr><tr><td><code>const_cast&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>Compile time</td><td>Remove/add <code>const</code> — e.g. calling a legacy API that forgot <code>const</code>. Modifying a truly const object is UB</td></tr><tr><td><code>reinterpret_cast&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>None</td><td>Reinterpret bits: pointer ↔ integer, unrelated pointer types. Platform-specific</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i><code>static_cast</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nenum class Level { Low = 1, High = 3 };\nint main() {\n    double avg = static_cast&lt;double&gt;(7) / 2;\n    int lvl = static_cast&lt;int&gt;(Level::High);\n    char c = static_cast&lt;char&gt;(65);\n    void* vp = &amp;lvl;\n    int* ip = static_cast&lt;int*&gt;(vp);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; avg &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; lvl &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; *ip &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3.5 3 A 3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield-check\"></i><code>dynamic_cast</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;typeinfo&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nstruct Animal { virtual ~Animal() = default; };     // must be polymorphic\nstruct Dog : Animal { void fetch() const { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"fetching\\n\"; } };\nstruct Cat : Animal {};\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;Animal&gt;&gt; zoo;\n    zoo.push_back(std::make_unique&lt;Dog&gt;());\n    zoo.push_back(std::make_unique&lt;Cat&gt;());\n    for (const auto&amp; a : zoo) {\n        if (auto* d = dynamic_cast&lt;Dog*&gt;(a.get())) d-&gt;fetch();     // nullptr if not a Dog\n        else std::cout &lt;&lt; \"not a dog\\n\";\n    }\n    Cat cat;\n    Animal&amp; ref = cat;\n    try {\n        Dog&amp; d = dynamic_cast&lt;Dog&amp;&gt;(ref);                         // reference form throws\n        d.fetch();\n    } catch (const std::bad_cast&amp;) {\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"bad_cast\\n\";\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>fetching\nnot a dog\nbad_cast</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Needing lots of <code>dynamic_cast</code> is a design smell — usually a virtual function should do the type-specific work. It also costs a run-time walk of the type hierarchy.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-id\"></i><code>typeid</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;typeinfo&gt;\nstruct Base { virtual ~Base() = default; };\nstruct Derived : Base {};\nstruct NonPoly {};\nstruct NonPolyDerived : NonPoly {};\nint main() {\n    Derived d;\n    Base&amp; b = d;\n    NonPolyDerived npd;\n    NonPoly&amp; np = npd;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (typeid(b) == typeid(Derived)) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (typeid(np) == typeid(NonPolyDerived)) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; typeid(int).name() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; typeid(double).name() &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // implementation-defined names\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 0\ni d</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i><code>const_cast</code> &amp; <code>reinterpret_cast</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cstdint&gt;\n#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nvoid legacyPrint(char* s) { std::cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }   // doesn't modify, but isn't const-correct\n\nint main() {\n    const char* msg = \"hello\";\n    legacyPrint(const_cast&lt;char*&gt;(msg));                 // OK only because legacyPrint doesn't write\n\n    int value = 0x01020304;\n    auto addr = reinterpret_cast&lt;std::uintptr_t&gt;(&amp;value);   // pointer → integer\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (addr % alignof(int)) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    float f = 1.0f;\n    std::uint32_t bits;\n    std::memcpy(&amp;bits, &amp;f, sizeof bits);                 // the safe way to inspect bits (or std::bit_cast)\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::hex &lt;&lt; bits &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>hello\n0\n3f800000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>*reinterpret_cast&lt;std::uint32_t*&gt;(&amp;f)</code> violates <em>strict aliasing</em> (undefined behaviour). Use <code>std::memcpy</code> or C++20 <code>std::bit_cast&lt;std::uint32_t&gt;(f)</code> to reinterpret an object's bytes.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why avoid C-style casts in C++?</summary><div><p><code>(T)x</code> silently tries <code>const_cast</code>, <code>static_cast</code> and <code>reinterpret_cast</code> combinations until one compiles, so it can cast away <code>const</code> or reinterpret pointers by accident. Named casts state intent, are restricted to what they say, and are easy to grep for.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>static_cast vs dynamic_cast for down-casting?</summary><div><p><code>static_cast&lt;Derived*&gt;(basePtr)</code> trusts you: no run-time check, UB if the object isn't really a <code>Derived</code>. <code>dynamic_cast</code> checks the actual type using RTTI and returns <code>nullptr</code> (or throws for references) when it's wrong — safer but slower, and it only works on polymorphic types.</p></div></details></section>"}